\subsection{Research attempt}
\paragraph{Model, date and investigated scope.}
OpenAI GPT-6 Astra (configured model \texttt{gpt-6-astra}), ultra reasoning; generated 11 October 2026. Proved that full nonzero-residue Fourier support forces equal masses and checked how dynamics removes an alternating-mass circular candidate. General equal-shift choreographies remain unresolved.

\paragraph{Sources and literature check.}
Read the supplied complete definition, catalogue wording, scope qualifications and source list for this canonical ID. The original-source citations are inherited as supplied references; no fresh primary-source or novelty search was performed for this attempt. No current open-status claim is inferred from this calculation.

\paragraph{A Fourier obstruction from center of mass.}Write an equal-shift choreography as \(q_i(t)=q(t+iP/n)\), \(0\le i<n\), and expand its smooth periodic trace in Fourier coefficients \(\widehat q_\ell\). In an inertial frame its center of mass is constant, so every nonzero temporal frequency satisfies
\[\widehat q_\ell\sum_{i=0}^{n-1}m_i e^{2\pi i\ell i/n}=0.
\]
If for each nonzero residue \(r\pmod n\) at least one coefficient \(\widehat q_\ell\ne0\) occurs with \(\ell\equiv r\), all nonconstant discrete Fourier coefficients of the mass vector vanish. Inverting the finite Fourier transform then forces every mass to equal their average. This proves a conditional obstruction requiring only the center-of-mass equation.
\paragraph{An inactive-mode test.}For a circular trace only frequencies \(\pm1\) occur. With four bodies, alternating masses \((a,b,a,b)\), \(a,b>0\), satisfy the center-of-mass condition. Newton's equations add an obstruction: at an a-mass vertex of radius \(R\), the required angular frequency is \(\omega^2=(a/4+b/\sqrt2)/R^3\); at a b-mass vertex it is \((b/4+a/\sqrt2)/R^3\). Equality implies \((a-b)(1/4-1/\sqrt2)=0\), hence \(a=b\). Thus passing the Fourier test is not sufficient.
\paragraph{Gap.}For a general trace with missing Fourier residues, one must use the full force equations to eliminate the remaining mass modes or construct a genuine counterexample. No such argument is proved here for arbitrary n. The source's historical small-n context is not treated as a new proof, and unequal phase shifts remain the distinct question of record 20002541.

\paragraph{Verification and adversarial audit.}
Checked center-of-mass Fourier factors, finite transform inversion, circular missing modes and both alternating-square force coefficients. This was an LLM adversarial audit, not independent human verification; no theorem prover was used. Prompts A then B applied to this ID: Checked center-of-mass Fourier factors, finite transform inversion, circular missing modes and both alternating-square force coefficients. Scope remains limited: Use the complete Newtonian force equations to control mass Fourier modes invisible to the trace frequencies.

Coordinator checked the choreography Fourier mass equations and alternating-mass square force comparison. This additional check was model-assisted, not independent human verification.

\paragraph{Tools, process and contributions.}
Python 3 and local shell used to read supplied files and serialize this manuscript; mathematics derived symbolically in the text, with no numerical experiment or formal proof assistant. Contribution prompt A was followed by prompt B. GPT-6 Astra with ultra reasoning drafted the research and performed its stated self-audit; any additional coordinator contribution is identified in the verification and audit. A GPT-6-based Codex coordinator assembled the manuscript and checked repository integration; its exact variant and reasoning configuration were not exposed. No human mathematical contribution, independent human proof check or formal proof certification is supplied. Complete derivations and the consulted source references are included here; private reasoning transcripts are not published.

